<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berat is a city in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> on the Osum river, dominated by a hilltop citadel. Contested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, Angevins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, it withstood an Angevin siege in 1281. It was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaka-family">Muzaka family</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1417, and its Ottoman-era quarters of white houses form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berat.jpg" alt="Berat" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arianit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berat_57.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ceramic finds of the seventh century BCE attest a settlement of the rocky hill, and Berat has been identified with ancient Antipatrea. In 200 BCE, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> reports, the Roman legatus Lucius Apustius stormed it, killed the men of military age, demolished the walls and burned it. In the early Byzantine period it was known as Pulcheriopolis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presian-i">Presian I</a> took it in the ninth century and gave it the Slavic name Beligrad, White City, from which Berat comes. The Bulgarian governor surrendered it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> in 1018, and the Second Bulgarian Empire retook it in 1203.</p><p>In 1274 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> recaptured Berat from Charles I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. In 1280 and 1281 Sicilian forces under Hugh the Red of Sully besieged it, and in March 1281 a relief force from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> drove them off. The town passed to the Serbian Empire in 1345 and was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1385. By 1396 the Albanian Muzaka family held it as the capital of the Principality of Berat, and in 1417 it became part of the Ottoman Empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under early Ottoman rule Berat declined, with only 710 houses by the end of the sixteenth century, but it recovered in the seventeenth and became a major centre of wood carving. From 1670 it was Muslim-majority. In the nineteenth century it took a leading part in the Albanian national revival, and revolts in 1833 and 1839 besieged the Ottoman forces in its castle. The Council of National Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> held its second session there in October 1944.</p><p>Berat is the seat of Berat County, on the Osum river, surrounded by mountains including Tomorr, a national park. It was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2008 for an architecture combining the influences of several civilisations, with an old fortified city of churches and mosques decorated with murals and frescoes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It served as a bridgehead for Charles's planned attack on Byzantium, but the Angevin army was defeated at the siege of <b>Berat</b> in 1281.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-albania-medieval">Kingdom of Albania (medieval)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Berat</b> itself was taken by the Serbian Empire in the 1340s and only came firmly into Muzaka hands around 1372.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-berat">Principality of Berat</a></li></ul></section>
